  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure builds back into the region today in the wake of \na cold front that has ushered in a slightly cooler airmass, so \nexpect temperatures to only max out in the mid to upper 40s for \nthe mountains and foothills, and in the low 50s to the south. \nHigh pressure then becomes centered directly overhead tonight \nwhich is going to lead to another night of strong radiational \ncooling. This pattern has been so persistent lately that the NBM\n(which usually doesn't handle radiational cooling all that \nwell) actually looks pretty reasonable, but MOS has still been \nincorporated into some of the better radiating locations to add\nsome value there. The result is likely one of the colder nights\nwe have seen so far with widespread 20s and upper teens (low \nteens at the better radiators) expected by early Wednesday \nmorning. \n\nHigh pressure than moves its way offshore Wednesday with return\nflow starting a warming trend that will last through the end of\nthe work week. Wednesday is likely going to see temperatures \ntop out in the 50s and rising dewpoints making for a warmer \nnight in the 30s. High astrotides are going to push this \nmorning's and Wednesday morning's high tides close to minor \nflood stage, but calm conditions and off shore winds are \nexpected to result in little to no surge meaning they should \nstay below. By Thursday temperatures should be able to mix in to\nthe 70s across the area. Dewpoints also continue to rise, so \nThursday night looks to end up relatively mild with temperatures\nlikely only being able to fall into the mid to upper 40s. \nClouds and a few showers are possible Thursday night ahead of \nthe next frontal passage and while global models have been \nconsistently showing a signal for more widespread shower \nchances, as this event moves into the hi- res window the \nsuggestion is for more widely scattered showers. So, while this \ndoes technically mean many locations will see a higher chance \nfor rain then they have seen over the last week or so, that \nchance is still going to be quite low. In fact the latest NBM, \nstill doesn't even really want to bring PoPs south of the \nmountains, but again as more hi- res model data gets ingested \nlow chances will likely begin expanding southward.\n\nThe front likely begins crossing early Friday morning with the \ncolder air filling in north of the mountains by the afternoon \nand limiting the temperatures in those locations to the mid to \nupper 50s. South of the mountains should be able to see \ntemperatures warm into the mid to upper 60s (maybe even 70 on \nthe coastal plain) before the front crosses. High pressure then \nmoves back overhead for Halloweekend which looks decent as this \nfrontal passage doesn't quite pack the punch of the current one,\nso daytime temperatures look to end up in the upper 50s and low\n60s. For Saturday night temperatures look to generally be in \nthe 40s and 50s during trick or treat time. For what it's worth \nglobal models have been showing a pretty consistent signal for \nNovember to begin wet, so that will be the window to watch for \nour next chance of potentially appreciable rainfall.",
